          "secNumber": "1.1",
          "heading": "Statistical Experiments, Probability Spaces, and Mathematical Modeling Foundations",
          "content": "At the heart of mathematical statistics lies the formal concept of a statistical experiment. In pure probability theory, one begins with a completely specified probability space $(\\Omega, \\mathcal{F}, P)$, where $\\Omega$ represents the sample space of all conceivable outcomes, $\\mathcal{F}$ is a $\\sigma$-algebra of events (subsets of $\\Omega$ satisfying closure under complementation and countable unions), and $P: \\mathcal{F} \\to [0, 1]$ is a known, fixed probability measure satisfying Kolmogorov's axioms: non-negativity, unit measure ($P(\\Omega) = 1$), and countable additivity over pairwise disjoint collections of events. In that deductive framework, probability theory investigates the mathematical consequences of a known data-generating law, characterizing the behavior of random variables, deriving marginal and conditional distributions, and establishing asymptotic limit theorems.\n\nMathematical statistics fundamentally reverses this epistemological direction. In statistical inference, the physical or experimental reality generates an observed data vector $\\mathbf{x} = (x_1, x_2, \\dots, x_n) \\in \\mathcal{X}$, where $\\mathcal{X}$ is the observation space (frequently a Borel measurable subset of Euclidean space $\\mathbb{R}^n$). Crucially, the true probability measure $P$ governing the generation of $\\mathbf{x}$ is unknown to the investigator. The primary objective of mathematical statistics is to make rigorous, mathematically justifiable assertions regarding the nature of the true, unobserved probability measure based exclusively on the realized observation $\\mathbf{x}$.\n\nFormally, a **statistical model** (or statistical experiment) is defined as a triplet:\n\n$$\\mathcal{E} = (\\mathcal{X}, \\mathcal{A}, \\mathcal{P})$$\n\nwhere:\n1. $\\mathcal{X}$ denotes the sample space of possible data realizations.\n2. $\\mathcal{A}$ denotes a designated $\\sigma$-algebra of observable events defined on $\\mathcal{X}$, typically chosen as the Borel $\\sigma$-algebra $\\mathcal{B}(\\mathcal{X})$ generated by the open topologies of Euclidean space.\n3. $\\mathcal{P} = \\{P_\\theta : \\theta \\in \\Theta\\}$ represents a specified family or collection of candidate probability measures defined on the measurable space $(\\mathcal{X}, \\mathcal{A})$, indexed by an arbitrary index set $\\Theta$.\n\nThe statistical model $\\mathcal{E}$ represents an explicit mathematical hypothesis formulated by the analyst. It asserts that the real-world random mechanism generating the data corresponds to some specific member $P_{\\theta^*} \\in \\mathcal{P}$, where $\\theta^* \\in \\Theta$ is the unknown \"true\" state of nature. The foundational challenge of estimation, hypothesis testing, and decision theory consists in identifying, approximating, or delimiting this true index $\\theta^*$ from the empirical evidence provided by the realization $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathcal{X}$.\n\nIf the family $\\mathcal{P}$ cannot be indexed by a finite-dimensional Euclidean vector, the model is classified as **nonparametric**. For instance, if $\\mathcal{P}$ is the collection of all continuous cumulative distribution functions on the real line $\\mathbb{R}$, the parameter space $\\Theta$ is an infinite-dimensional function space. If $\\mathcal{P}$ involves both finite-dimensional parameters of structural interest (such as a treatment effect) and infinite-dimensional nuisance parameters (such as an arbitrary baseline hazard function in survival analysis), the model is designated as **semiparametric**. In this foundational volume of mathematical statistics, we concentrate principally on **parametric statistical models**, wherein the candidate measures are indexed by a finite-dimensional parameter vector $\\boldsymbol{\\theta} \\in \\Theta \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^k$.",
