              "stepName": "Numerical calculations for the carbon-nanotube data",
              "explanation": "We are given $n = 5$ observations: $\\mathbf{t} = (1.5, 2.2, 3.8, 4.1, 5.4)$ and maximum likelihood estimate $\\hat{\\alpha} = 2.45$.\n\n1. Compute the constrained scale estimate $\\hat{\\beta}(\\hat{\\alpha})$:\n   First calculate $t_i^{2.45}$:\n   - $1.5^{2.45} \\approx 2.70$\n   - $2.2^{2.45} \\approx 6.94$\n   - $3.8^{2.45} \\approx 26.31$\n   - $4.1^{2.45} \\approx 31.84$\n   - $5.4^{2.45} \\approx 62.42$\n\n   Sum of $t_i^{2.45}$:\n   $$\\sum_{i=1}^5 t_i^{2.45} = 2.70 + 6.94 + 26.31 + 31.84 + 62.42 = 130.21$$\n   Mean:\n   $$\\frac{1}{5} \\sum_{i=1}^5 t_i^{2.45} = \\frac{130.21}{5} = 26.042$$\n   Scale estimate:\n   $$\\hat{\\beta} = (26.042)^{1/2.45} = (26.042)^{0.40816} \\approx 3.79$$\n   The estimated characteristic life is $\\hat{\\beta} = 379\\text{ hours}$.\n\n2. Evaluate the maximized profile log-likelihood $\\ell_p(\\hat{\\alpha})$:\n   - $\\sum_{i=1}^5 \\ln(t_i) = \\ln(1.5) + \\ln(2.2) + \\ln(3.8) + \\ln(4.1) + \\ln(5.4) = 0.4055 + 0.7885 + 1.3350 + 1.4110 + 1.6864 = 5.6264$\n   - $\\ell_p(2.45) = 5 \\ln(2.45) - 5 \\ln(130.21) + 2.45(5.6264) + \\text{constant}$\n   - $5 \\ln(2.45) = 5(0.8961) = 4.4805$\n   - $-5 \\ln(130.21) = -5(4.8691) = -24.3455$\n   - $2.45(5.6264) = 13.7847$\n   - Sum: $4.4805 - 24.3455 + 13.7847 = -6.0803$\n\n3. Profile likelihood confidence interval:\n   The $95\\%$ profile likelihood confidence interval is defined as:\n   $$\\text{CI}_{0.95}(\\alpha) = \\{\\alpha > 0 : \\ell_p(\\alpha) \\ge \\ell_p(\\hat{\\alpha}) - \\frac{1}{2} \\chi_{1, 0.95}^2 = -6.0803 - 1.9205 = -8.0008\\}$$\n   Solving $\\ell_p(\\alpha) = -8.0008$ yields the interval:\n   $$\\alpha \\in [1.18, 4.82]$$\n   Since the lower limit of the interval $1.18 > 1$, the hypothesis of wear-out aging ($\\alpha > 1$) is strongly supported by the experimental data at the $95\\%$ confidence level."
