              "stepName": "Reciprocal Quantile Relationship for Upper and Lower Tails",
              "explanation": "Let $F \\sim F_{\\nu_1, \\nu_2}$. By definition of the critical value $F_{\\alpha, \\nu_1, \\nu_2}$, the upper-tail probability is:\n\n$$P\\left( F \\ge F_{\\alpha, \\nu_1, \\nu_2} \\right) = \\alpha$$\n\nEquivalently, the lower-tail probability is:\n\n$$P\\left( F \\le F_{1 - \\alpha, \\nu_1, \\nu_2} \\right) = \\alpha$$\n\nNow invert the inequality inside the probability:\n\n$$P\\left( F \\le F_{1 - \\alpha, \\nu_1, \\nu_2} \\right) = P\\left( \\frac{1}{F} \\ge \\frac{1}{F_{1 - \\alpha, \\nu_1, \\nu_2}} \\right) = \\alpha$$\n\nFrom Section 3.9, the reciprocal variable follows an inverted degrees-of-freedom distribution:\n\n$$\\frac{1}{F} \\sim F_{\\nu_2, \\nu_1}$$\n\nBy definition of the upper-tail quantile for $F_{\\nu_2, \\nu_1}$:\n\n$$P\\left( F_{\\nu_2, \\nu_1} \\ge F_{\\alpha, \\nu_2, \\nu_1} \\right) = \\alpha$$\n\nComparing the two statements:\n\n$$\\frac{1}{F_{1 - \\alpha, \\nu_1, \\nu_2}} = F_{\\alpha, \\nu_2, \\nu_1} \\implies F_{1 - \\alpha, \\nu_1, \\nu_2} = \\frac{1}{F_{\\alpha, \\nu_2, \\nu_1}}$$\n\nThis identity is of great practical utility: standard published statistical tables tabulate only upper-tail critical values (e.g., $\\alpha = 0.05, 0.01$). To find a lower-tail critical value for $F_{\\nu_1, \\nu_2}$, an analyst simply takes the reciprocal of the upper-tail critical value with the degrees of freedom swapped ($F_{\\alpha, \\nu_2, \\nu_1}$)."
